  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n\nThe central idea is to solve the problem using a **progressive resolution optimization (PRO)** approach. Unlike traditional methods that fix a resolution early, this strategy begins with a coarse discretization (low-resolution) and iteratively refines it (increasing resolution) while optimizing along each step. The core rationale is:\n\n- Coarse resolution optimization is computationally cheaper and can locate promising but not necessarily optimal solutions faster\n- Progressive refinement helps escape shallow local optima in higher resolutions by leveraging solutions from adjacent lower resolutions\n- Coarse-to-fine progression reduces computational burden in high dimensions by examining simpler representations first\n- The method strategically increases resolution only when improvements are detected, balancing exploration and exploitation\n- Progressive resolution applies a multi-scale analysis principle common in mathematical optimization and image processing\n\nThis approach differs from the parent in its systematic resolution increase coupled with adaptive stopping conditions and solution interpolation between grids. The key limitation is that we assume the optimal solution exhibits some scale invariance properties, which may not hold for all problem instances. The solution process is distinguished from other strategies by its: progressive resolution framework without prior basis construction, and its focus on empirical improvement signals rather than theoretical guarantees.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n\nThe problem is defined with variables:\n- n: number of samples (resolution)\n- h: f an array of length n, with 0 \u2264 h\u1d62 \u2264 1 for all i, and \u222bh dx = 1\n- x: domain [0,2] with dx = 2/n\n\nObjective: minimize C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx over k\n\nConstraints:\n1. 0 \u2264 h\u1d62 \u2264 1\n2. \u222bh dx = 1\n\nFor PRO, we introduce artificial variables:\n- \u03b1: Coarse-to-fine ratio (initial resolution divisor)\n- \u03b2: Resolution multiplier (increment factor)\n- \u03b4k: Improvement threshold for upscaling\n- \u03b8: Minimum iteration count per resolution\n\nThe resolution transformation is defined as:\nh\u1d62 \u2192 h\u0303(x\u2099) = interpolated(h, new grid)\nwhere interpolation preserves integral value\n\nDerivatives are approximated using:\n\u2202C\u2085/\u2202h = [\u2202/\u2202h \u222bh(1-h(\u00b7+k))dx] computed via finite differences\n\nConstraints are enforced using projection:\nh\u0303 \u2190 clip(h\u0303, 0, 1)\nh\u0303 \u2190 normalize_integrand(map=h\u0303)\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n\nfunction run(seed=42,budget_s=1100):\n  Initialize n_low = 50 if parent provided else 400\n  Set n_high_factor = 2.0\n  Set n_min = max(floor(n_low/5), 30)\n  Set n_win = min(max(floor(n_low/2), 100), n_high_factor*300)\n\n  Determine parent solution:\n  if initial_h_values exists:\n    h0 = initial_h_values.copy()\n    n0 = len(h0)\n    h_parent = h0\n  else:\n    h_parent = np.ones(n_min)*0.5\n\n  Feasibility projection:\n  h_parent = project_feasible(h_parent, n_min)\n\n  Progressive resolution steps:\n  1. Find optimal solution at fixed resolution using gradient descent with:\n      - LBFGS-B optimizer\n      - Barzilai-Borwein method for step-size selection\n      - Cubic line search with integral preservation\n  2. Monitor: If improvement percentage < 0.1%, reduce resolution\n  3. If no resolution increase threshold reached, increase n by factor 1.5\n  4. L\u00f6sungsinterpolation mit adaptivem Frobenius-Tensor-Ist-Zustand\n  5. Re-evaluate correlation at the target resolution\n  6. Track best solution found so far\n\n  Progressive resolution algorithm:\n\n  n_resolution = n_min\n  best_sol = None\n  best_c5 = float('inf')\n\n  while n_resolution <= max_resolution and budget > 300:\n      t_start = time.time()\n      # Optimization phase\n      h_opt = lbfgsb_optimize(h_parent, n_resolution, seed)\n      c5_val = compute_c5(h_opt, n_resolution)\n\n      if c5_val < best_c5:\n          best_sol = h_opt.copy()\n          best_c5 = c5_val\n\n      # Check for resolution increase\n      time_remaining = budget - (time.time() - t_start)\n      if time_remaining < 100: continue\n\n      n_resolution = min(max(n_resolution*1.5, n_resolution+10), 600)\n      if best_c5_improvement < 0.0001: # Adaptative threshold\n          continue\n      h_parent = project_feasible(interpolate_solution(best_sol, n_resolution), n_resolution)\n\n  Return (best_sol, best_c5, n_resolution)\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n\nDefault configuration:\n\n  - Coarse resolution: 50 points\n  - Max resolution: 600 points\n  - Game theory-inspired alpha schedule: exponentially decayed steps\n  - Implementation: gradient-based optimization using LBFGS-B with projectors\n  - Alpha trajectory: {100, 500, 1000} for barrier parameters\n  - Sample selection: simulated annealing using top 3% improvements at each resolution\n  - Line search: cubic interpolation with integral preservation\n\nThe included strategy indicates optimal parameter range for resolution increase [1.4, 1.6] based on empirical observation. Our implementation follows a 'greedy' variant in resolution increase only when computational budget allows.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n\nDominant cost components:\n- Gradient computation: O(n) per function evaluation\n- LBFGS-B iterations: typically 500 per resolution level\n- Interpolation: O(n log n) due to post-facto projection\n\nEstimated time profiles:\nn=100: ~12 seconds\nn=200: ~45 seconds\nn=300: ~90 seconds\nn=400: ~180 seconds\nn=500: ~350 seconds\n\nSuggested runtime schedule:\n\nbudget_s = 1100:\n\n  - Coarse phase (n=50): 500 seconds\n  - Medium phase (n=150): 500 seconds\n  - Fine phase (n=400): 600 seconds\n\nWithin each resolution, allocate 70% to optimization and 30% to evaluation.\n\nFallback: In case of optimizer failure, fall back to simplex method with progressive resolution increase. The algorithm must validate solution feasibility at each step.\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n\nvalidate_final_solution(h, n):\n  dx = 2.0/n\n  integral = np.sum(h) * dx\n  assert(np.all(0 <= h <= 1)), \"Bound violation\"\n  assert(np.allclose(integral, 1.0, atol=1e-3)), \"Integral not preserved\"\n  c5 = compute_c5(h, n)\n  return round(c5, 6), integral\n\nStrict validation checks:\n- Integral constraint: should be 1 with tolerance \u00b11e-3\n- Bounds: all h[i] \u2208 [0,1] with tolerance \u00b11e-6\n- No NaN/Inf values\n- Positive definite solution matrix check\n\nReturn value contract:\n- h_values: numpy array of length n_points\n- c5_bound: float value rounded to 6 decimal places\n- n_points: integer >0\n\nThe function returns the solution only after passing validation. Candidates from any resolution level may be considered if they pass these strict checks.",
